---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="tcplot Online: Feedback Session | tcplot: Data Visualization in Minneapolis and St. Paul"
    description="Bring a chart you're working on to a structured online feedback hour, and leave knowing what it actually communicates."
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-8 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">
                Upcoming: tcplot Online Feedback Session
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Wednesday, Sep 30 · 7:00 PM to 8:00 PM CDT
                </p>
                <p class="mt-1">
                    Online, on Google Meet. The link goes out to everyone
                    who RSVPs on Meetup shortly before we start.
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Bring a chart. Leave with a room full of careful readers on it.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Our first online session is a structured feedback hour for
                charts in progress. Bring anything you're working on: a
                dashboard for work, a personal project, a piece from One
                Dataset, Many Interpretations, or something you sketched that
                afternoon. No advance submission; just show up, and share
                your screen when it's your turn.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                The point of the structure below is to get you the feedback
                that's hardest to get anywhere else: not whether people like
                your chart, but what it actually communicates.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                How the feedback works
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Each chart gets about twelve minutes, timeboxed, in five
                steps:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-6">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Set the frame (1 minute).</span
                        > The presenter shares the chart and says two things:
                        who it's for, and the one question they want
                        answered.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Silent looking (1 minute).</span
                        ></span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >What do you see? (4 minutes).</span
                        > Readers describe what the chart communicated: what
                        they read first, what the main message seemed to be,
                        where they got lost. No suggestions yet. This round
                        tells the presenter what the chart does, not what
                        anyone thinks of it.</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >What would you change? (4 minutes).</span
                        > Now suggestions, aimed at the presenter's question
                        first. Specific beats sweeping: "the legend made me
                        re-read the axis" helps more than "simplify it."</span
                    >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="mt-1 text-gray-400">&#x25B8;</span>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >Presenter gets the last word (1 minute).</span
                        > What they're taking away, and what they're setting
                        aside. Both are allowed; it's their chart.</span
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                What to bring
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                A chart you can share on screen, at any stage: polished,
                half-built, or paper held up to the camera.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                No chart? Come anyway. Feedback needs readers more than it
                needs another presenter, and the "what do you see" round is
                where fresh eyes matter most.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Who this is for
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Anyone making charts, in any tool, at any level. The protocol
                is designed so a first chart and a fiftieth chart get the same
                quality of reading.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                RSVP on <a
                    href="https://www.meetup.com/tcplot/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Meetup</a
                > to get the link.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
